<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indonesian independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indonesian independence was proclaimed in 1945 and recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1949 after a period of armed conflict and negotiation. It ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>. The loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> was the decisive blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence.jpg" alt="Indonesian independence" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Mendur (also Frans Mendoer) (1913 – 1971) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indonesia_declaration_of_independence_17_August_1945.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> authorities set up a partly elected People's Council, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksraad-dutch-east-indies">Volksraad</a>, which gave Indonesian nationalists a voice for the first time. In 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> turned his study club into the Indonesian Nationalist Association, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-party">Indonesian National Party</a> in May 1928, aiming at independence through mass non-cooperation. Sukarno was arrested in 1929 and again in 1933, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir">Sutan Sjahrir</a> were exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boven-digoel">Boven Digul</a> camp, which effectively ended the non-cooperation movement.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> in January 1942, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> surrendered on 8 March. Seeking to mobilise the population, the Japanese freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> from exile in 1943, and he and Hatta cooperated with them while keeping independence as their aim. On 7 September 1944 Prime Minister Kuniaki Koiso promised independence for the East Indies later on. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> were bombed, Sukarno and Hatta were flown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-lat">Dalat</a> on 9 August 1945, where Field Marshal Hisaichi Terauchi promised independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> surrendered on 15 August 1945. Younger activists, the pemuda, urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and Hatta to declare independence at once and without the Japanese-formed Preparatory Committee. When the two refused, pemuda leaders took them on 16 August to Rengasdengklok, but they still would not proclaim without assurances from the Japanese. Achmad Soebardjo brought them back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, and they met committee members at the house of Rear-Admiral Tadashi Maeda.</p><p>Sjahrir's pemuda had drafted a text declaring that government organs would be seized by the people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and Hatta rejected it as likely to spark violence, and Sukarno drafted a version speaking of an orderly transfer of power in the shortest possible time. Only Sukarno and Hatta signed it. At 10:00 on 17 August 1945 Sukarno, with Hatta beside him, read the proclamation. Latief Hendraningat raised the red-and-white flag, and the attendees sang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Youths spread the news across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> by Japanese news and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> facilities, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> it was broadcast by radio. Many ordinary Indonesians doubted it or saw it as Japanese deception, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> only the republican elite of major cities knew of it by mid-September. On 18 August the committee elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> president and Hatta vice-president and ratified the constitution. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> regarded the republicans as collaborators with the Japanese and wished to restore colonial rule, which began a four-year war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> acknowledged Indonesian independence in 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Proclamation of Indonesian Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japanese occupation destroyed the East Indies empire in 1942, and two military offensives, the so-called Police Actions of 1947 and 1948–1949, failed to reverse <b>Indonesian independence</b>, proclaimed by the Republic in 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Indonesian independence</b> the sultanate was absorbed into the new state's administration around 1950; the royal family keeps a ceremonial role in North Maluku.</blockquote><a href="/state/bacan-sultanate">Bacan Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Indonesian independence</b> the sultanates of Sumbawa were dissolved, Dompu becoming a regency of West Nusa Tenggara in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/dompu-sultanate">Dompu Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Indonesian independence</b> Sultan Zainal Abidin Syah supported Indonesia's claim to West New Guinea and became its first governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
